Enabling AP Isolation turns on a digital wall between your Wi-Fi devices so they cannot see each other, while disabling it removes that wall so your devices can talk directly.
Here is what happens inside your router in both modes:

What Enabling AP Isolation Means ("Privacy Wall On")

• Internet Only: Every device connected to your Wi-Fi can still go online, browse websites, and use apps normally.
• Devices Are Invisible to Each Other: Your router blocks all direct data between connected devices. Your laptop cannot detect, ping, or send files to your phone, and your phone cannot reach your laptop.
• Protects Against Local Attacks: If a stranger or an infected device joins your Wi-Fi, they cannot scan or hack your personal devices on that same network.

What Disabling AP Isolation Means ("Direct Sharing On")

• Devices Can See Each Other: Your router allows devices on the same Wi-Fi to discover and communicate directly with one another.
• Enables Local Tools & Features: Your laptop can scan your phone with nmap, your phone can send web traffic to Burp Suite on your laptop, and you can cast videos to a TV or print over Wi-Fi.
• Requires Network Trust: Because devices can talk directly, you should only disable AP Isolation on private networks where you trust everyone connected.
