AP Isolation (Access Point Isolation) is a Wi-Fi security setting that prevents devices on the same network from seeing or communicating with each other. When enabled, your laptop and phone can both access the internet, but they cannot detect, ping, or send data directly to one another.

How to Turn Off AP Isolation

Your earlier scan showed your gateway is ://baseline.com (192.168.1.1), which is a cellular/LTE CPE router. Follow these steps to disable AP Isolation:
1. Open the Router Page: Open your web browser on Ubuntu and go to http://192.168.1.1.
2. Sign In: Enter the admin username and password (usually found on the label underneath or behind the CPE router).
3. Go to Wi-Fi Settings: Look for Wi-Fi, WLAN, or Wireless in the main menu, then open Advanced Settings or Security.
4. Disable Isolation: Find AP Isolation, Client Isolation, or User Isolation, switch it to Disabled / Off, and click Save or Apply.

Alternative: Use a Mobile Hotspot

• Quick Workaround: If your CPE router is locked by your internet provider and does not let you change AP Isolation, turn on the Wi-Fi Hotspot on your phone and connect your Ubuntu laptop to it.
• Why It Works: Phone hotspots normally allow local traffic between the phone and connected laptops, letting nmap and Burp Suite communicate immediately.
